<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Three Seals Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Three Seals Code, Kotmai Tra Sam Duang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Three Seals Law is a collection of law texts compiled in 1805 on the orders of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> of Siam, most of them laws of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> era that had survived the destruction of Ayutthaya in 1767. After a divorce case made the king suspect that the laws had been altered, a commission of judges, scribes and officials revised them in 11 months, producing 27 laws in 41 volumes, each stamped with the seals of the three ministries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatthai">Mahatthai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalahom">Kalahom</a> and Phrakhlang. It remained the working law of Siam until modern codes partly replaced it from 1908. Under a 1978 Supreme Court ruling, its parts not since replaced are still in force.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/three-seals-law.jpg" alt="Three Seals Law" width="330" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Librovore · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangkok_National_Museum_2024-06-01_(038).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rama I had the surviving laws of Ayutthaya revised into the <b>Three Seals Law</b> of 1805 and sponsored a council of monks to revise the Buddhist canon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the sakdina system, which King Borommatrailokkanat fixed in 1454 and the <b>Three Seals Law</b> records, each rank carried a number, in name the rai of land its holder was entitled to: 5 for a slave, 20 for a commoner, 600 for a learned monk, 10,000 for a…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ayutthaya">Social Hierarchy of the Ayutthaya Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
